// Title: Suspicious IIS URL GlobalRules Rewrite Via AppCmd
// Sigma ID: 7c8af9b2-dcae-41a2-a9db-b28c288b5f08
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2023-01-22 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth
// Description: Detects usage of "appcmd" to create new global URL rewrite rules. This behaviour has been
//   observed being used by threat actors to add new rules so they can access their webshells.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate usage of appcmd to add new URL rewrite rules
// References:
//   - https://twitter.com/malmoeb/status/1616702107242971144
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/739120/how-to-add-re-write-global-rule-with-action-type-r
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_iis_appcmd_susp_rewrite_rule.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\appcmd.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "appcmd.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "set" and ProcessCommandLine contains "config" and ProcessCommandLine contains "section:system.webServer/rewrite/globalRules" and ProcessCommandLine contains "commit:")
